 | Situation                                                                      | ASM (`+DATA`)                                                                                                                                     | File System (`/u01/oradata`)                                                         |
| ------------------------------------------------------------------------------ | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |
| Leave the file name empty                                                      | Works — Oracle creates an OMF name. Requires `DB_CREATE_FILE_DEST = '+DATA'`.                                                                     | Works — Oracle creates an OMF name. Requires `DB_CREATE_FILE_DEST = '/u01/oradata'`. |
| Give a normal name (e.g., `+DATA/system02.dbf` or `/u01/oradata/system02.dbf`) | Usually works — Oracle creates the file and may create an alias using the specified name. The ASM alias directory must exist if one is specified. | Works — Oracle creates the file using the specified path and name.                   |
| Give a full OMF-style name                                                     | Fails with `ORA-01276` when adding a new file.                                                                                                    | Fails with `ORA-01276` when adding a new file.                                       |

Why the difference? 
On ASM, almost every file is treated as an Oracle Managed File. Oracle is very strict — it does not allow you to invent names that look like OMF names. 
On File System, Oracle is more flexible. Even if DB_CREATE_FILE_DEST is set, you can still specify a normal file name (without the OMF pattern), and Oracle accepts it. 

 
